# xfs codecpack (WP16 container ABI v1.1): decomposes an XFS filesystem
# image into per-file members. Members are the regular files reachable
# from the root directory; idx = inode number (images holding a member
# with ino > 1048575 are declined -- MAX_IDX, the FS idx cap; this comment
# said 65535 until WP-XFS-CLASSIFY, which is the cap the pack shipped with
# before MAX_IDX was raised). The recipe carries
# every non-member byte verbatim (superblocks, AG headers, B+trees, whole
# inode chunks, directory blocks, the log, free space) plus the range
# table; rebuild splices member bytes back into their extents bit-exactly.
#
# The pack binary is C (libc only), built to bin/xfs (the pack-dir-relative
# argv[0] convention: probe() and the exec hooks both resolve "bin/xfs"
# against the pack directory); tools/test-xfs.sh builds it with
# cc -O2 -Wall -Wextra. Until then probe() simply skips the pack.
#
# Supported: sb v4/v5, blocksize 4096, inodes 256/512, EXTENTS and
# single-level-BTREE data forks, shortform/block/leaf directories.
# Declined: realtime, quota, rmapbt/reflink, nrext64, unknown feature
# bits, node-format directories, deeper bmap B+trees. See xfs.c.
name = xfs
type = container
algo = 19
codec_id = 19
pack_version = 1
version = 1
min_read = 1
generation = 1
family = container
category = primary
provides = container
replaces =
conflicts =
priority = 100
caps = container|external
dec_mem = 0
sniff.magic = 58465342
sniff.ext = xfs,xfsimg
enumerate = bin/xfs enumerate {in} {out}
extract = bin/xfs extract {in} {idx} {out}
strip = bin/xfs strip {in} {out}
rebuild = bin/xfs rebuild {recipe} {dir} {out}
# WP16b: the map command makes the pack SEEKABLE (CAP_SEEK): reads splice
# locally from the recipe + member siblings and never exec the pack.
map = bin/xfs map {in} {out}
# CLI-only manifest command, NOT an ABI command (the plugin ABI carries five;
# ext4fs declares its classify the same way). Emits the GCFG table: what every
# byte range of the image IS -- meta_structure vs garbage, and whether the
# pack READ the garbage and found it all zero (so it can be reproduced without
# being stored). The core must probe for this command and fall back to "no
# classification" when a pack lacks it -- packs in the wild predate it.
classify = bin/xfs classify {in} {out}

# WP-ABSORB: this pack's image is a FILESYSTEM that `invf-mkfs --absorb`
# may adopt in place. The core reads the sniff magic above to decide whether
# it claims an image, asks `classify` for the used-byte map, and reserves
# everything the map does not call garbage. absorb_name/absorb_check are
# optional: the name of the file the old image keeps inside the new volume,
# and a checker for a second opinion.
absorb = 1
absorb_name = old.xfs
# Opt into the FS-owned decomposition generation. This pack renders a v1
# (MRMP) map today, so the stamp is a no-op -- but a pack that emits v2
# without this key gets a map header whose generation is whatever the
# pack happened to write, and a later generation bump re-decomposes
# nothing. The engine only stamps an MRM2 header (src/core/vol_cpack.c,
# the map-header stamp in the container sweep), so declaring it here is
# inert for v1 and correct for v2. Matches the registry manifest.
decomp_gen = 1
# admission input: sum(member usize) + 64 MiB
estimate = bin/xfs estimate {in}
